<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelm Kaisen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wilhelm Kaisen (1887–1979) was a German Social Democratic politician who was mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and president of its senate from 1945 to 1965. He led the city's reconstruction after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and its re-establishment as a federal state in 1947. Under him Bremen served as the main supply port for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-kaisen.jpg" alt="Wilhelm Kaisen" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Senatspressestelle Bremen · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bgm_Kaisen_k.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carl Wilhelm Kaisen was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> on 22 May 1887 and grew up there; his family's poverty kept him from a higher education. He joined the Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1905 and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, his wife's home city. From 1919 to 1928 he was a journalist and editor-in-chief of the party newspaper Bremer Bürger-Zeitung, and from 1920 to 1928 a member of Bremen's parliament, the Bürgerschaft.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1928 Kaisen became the Social Democratic senator for social services in the Senate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, but in March 1933 the Nazis pressured him to resign. He took no part in politics under Nazi rule and worked on the land in the Bremen suburb of Borgfeld. On 1 August 1945 the US military government made him mayor. With the support of the Liberals and the Communists he set out to restore the political and economic structures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, and he was returned to office at the first free elections after the war. In 1958–59 he served as the tenth President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany">Bundesrat</a>.</p><p>Kaisen was popular with the general public but less so within his own party, and his support for placing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> led to a break with the party chairman Kurt Schumacher. He resigned as mayor in July 1965, after nearly twenty years in office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kaisen died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> on 19 December 1979. Though not well known in the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, he is held in high regard in Bremen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremerhaven">Bremerhaven</a> as a symbol of the reconstruction after 1945. The great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> bridge in the centre of Bremen, a high school in the Neustadt district, a container terminal in Bremerhaven and a rescue cruiser of the Maritime Search and Rescue Service were named after him, and there is a statue of him at Herdentor near Bremen's central station.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wilhelm Kaisen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1946" aria-label="Hamburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wilhelm Kaisen"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5506 5512h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5506 5512h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1946">What was here in 1946? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
